From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Diljit Singh <hey@djdev.me>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 07:40:12 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] soc: apple: rtkit: take a co-processor through a system sleep

A firmware-driven sleep powers the co-processors off, and RTKit had no
way to stop one for that or to bring it back without a full reboot of
its firmware. This adds what the drivers need, each step measured
against macOS:

- AP-only power notices. macOS tells the AOP, MTP and SMC about a sleep
  with the AP power state alone, 0x201 before it and 0x20 after, and
  leaves them running. A noirq variant polls.
- A sleep stop. After the IOP's idle ack macOS waits for the CPU status
  to read stopped and idle, clears bit 0 of the mailbox's I2A control
  word, and clears the run bit twice, each time from what it reads back.
  apple_rtkit_sleep_stop() does that, with a 5 ms poll for the status;
  on an error the run bit stays set and the firmware still runs.
- A polled idle for a stop that can't be taken back. ANS doesn't come
  back from idle with its power kept, so its idle follows every refusal,
  with interrupts off and one CPU up. The messages are sent and acked by
  polling, the syslog and IOReport notifications that want an ack are
  answered as they come, and OSLOG counters are echoed, below. A counter
  the receive work still owed, which can't run then, is echoed before
  the first state request, and whichever side takes it first echoes it,
  so none is echoed twice.
- A warm restart. After a real sleep the firmware restarts with its
  buffers and rings in place and sends HELLO unasked. Nothing is freed
  and no endpoint is started again; the endpoint map is echoed as macOS
  does, with bit 1 added to the first part, and a driver can set what
  the last part gets back (ANS gets endpoint 0x20 only).
  apple_rtkit_sleep_return() does that after a sleep that reached the
  hardware. After one that didn't, a firmware that idled gets -EAGAIN:
  an idled firmware doesn't wake with its power kept, so it stays down
  until the next real sleep restarts it. One whose idle failed still
  runs, and gets 0. apple_rtkit_sleep_return_start() is the first half,
  for a driver that starts other firmware before waiting, and
  apple_rtkit_power_cycled_return() starts one its driver power-cycled
  without a sleep.
- Hooks around the AP state change in boot and idle, for a driver that
  has to answer another port in between.
- A polled port that only answers the co-processor: the DCP's secure
  RTBuddy port, whose HELLO and endpoint map macOS's secure side echoes
  back unchanged.
- OSLOG counter echo. A firmware counts its log writes, holds the next
  counter until the last is echoed, and with one outstanding takes no AP
  power notice: a DCP restarted warm refuses its next AP idle, and the
  MTP and the SMC stopped answering after about eight sleeps. macOS
  echoes every one, so on T8140 every RTKit device's counters are
  echoed, and a driver can turn that off.
- A crash on a crash log endpoint the firmware never announced. Such a
  firmware asks for that buffer only when it crashes, at an address it
  chose. The request was taken for a boot-time one and dropped, so the
  driver never learned of the crash and kept sending to it: the AOP
  after a wake, refusing every later sleep. It's reported at once, and
  the buffer is mapped through the device's IOMMU when that's one
  contiguous range and acknowledged, so the log is dumped when it comes.

Management message 0xc, which follows the IOP's idle ack, is no longer
logged as unknown. An adopted session completes its endpoint map at
adoption, as its previous owner did that.

A handshake given up halfway can leave a firmware started that nothing
stops, so RTKit's waits end only on a fatal signal, and during a system
sleep not even that. The freezer signals every user task as a sleep
begins: on a J700 that cut a display power-on short as a sleep began,
left the DCP running while its driver took it as stopped, and every
later sleep was refused.

Rust bindings cover idle, mailbox off, warm restart and the OSLOG echo.

Nothing changes for a driver that doesn't call the new functions.

Signed-off-by: Diljit Singh <hey@djdev.me>

[j604 backport: kept our names and retained-wake work (IDLE_NOTIFY 0xc, coproc_places_buffers instead of Aurora's early_crashlog from b7069ac48f, wake_retained, 3-arg apple_rtkit_wait_timeout now also uninterruptible during suspend-to-mem and killable otherwise); added warm restart, secure port, poll_waits, polled sleep idle, sleep stop/return, boot/idle hooks (boot_common gets 4 args, wake_retained updated); OSLOG echo default stays apple,t8140 only, so T8132 behaviour is unchanged until a DS1 probe shows it is needed (TO-VERIFY)]
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5.5

---
 drivers/soc/apple/rtkit-internal.h            |  13 +
 drivers/soc/apple/rtkit.c                     | 591 +++++++++++++++++-
 include/linux/soc/apple/rtkit.h               | 104 +++
 rust/kernel/soc/apple/rtkit.rs                |  26 +
 tools/asahi/rtkit-oslog-tests.py              |   3 +
 .../selftests/drivers/gpu/apple-dcp/boot.py   |   2 +-
 6 files changed, 712 insertions(+), 27 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/soc/apple/rtkit-internal.h b/drivers/soc/apple/rtkit-internal.h
index 29345f069c..ec924a21c6 100644
--- a/drivers/soc/apple/rtkit-internal.h
+++ b/drivers/soc/apple/rtkit-internal.h
@@ -61,6 +61,17 @@ struct apple_rtkit {
 	 * hand out kdebug buffers, and poll the mailbox while waiting.
 	 */
 	bool sync_syslog_ack;
+	/* Echo each OSLOG channel-0 counter, as macOS does. */
+	bool oslog_echo;
+	/* A counter queued for the receive work and not yet echoed. */
+	u64 oslog_owed;
+	/* Device interrupts are off: poll the mailbox while waiting. */
+	bool poll_waits;
+	/* The firmware is restarting with its buffers and rings still in place. */
+	bool warm_restart;
+	u32 warm_last_mask;
+	/* A port that only answers the co-processor, see apple_rtkit_port_handshake(). */
+	bool port_only;
 	DECLARE_KFIFO(sync_ack_fifo, struct apple_mbox_msg, 64);
 	struct work_struct sync_ack_work;
 
@@ -89,6 +100,8 @@ struct apple_rtkit {
 	/* Session inherited from the bootloader: INIT geometry never observed. */
 	bool syslog_inherited;
 	bool crashlog_inherited;
+	/* The crash log buffer is the firmware's own, mapped here when it crashed. */
+	bool crashlog_placed;
 	bool oslog_inherited;
 
 	struct workqueue_struct *wq;
diff --git a/drivers/soc/apple/rtkit.c b/drivers/soc/apple/rtkit.c
index 8a6584c778..b82c9f9b70 100644
--- a/drivers/soc/apple/rtkit.c
+++ b/drivers/soc/apple/rtkit.c
@@ -6,10 +6,13 @@
 
 #include "rtkit-internal.h"
 
+#include <linux/iommu.h>
+#include <linux/iopoll.h>
 #include <linux/jiffies.h>
 #include <linux/of.h>
 #include <linux/overflow.h>
 #include <linux/sizes.h>
+#include <linux/suspend.h>
 
 enum {
 	APPLE_RTKIT_PWR_STATE_OFF = 0x00, /* power off, cannot be restarted */
@@ -61,6 +64,9 @@ enum {
 
 #define APPLE_RTKIT_MGMT_PWR_STATE GENMASK_ULL(15, 0)
 
+#define APPLE_RTKIT_POLLED_STEP_US 10
+#define APPLE_RTKIT_POLLED_LOOPS   20000
+
 #define APPLE_RTKIT_CRASHLOG_CRASH 1
 
 #define APPLE_RTKIT_BUFFER_REQUEST	1
@@ -115,6 +121,14 @@ enum {
 #define APPLE_RTKIT_CA_EVENT 2
 #define APPLE_RTKIT_CA_EVENT_ACK 3
 
+#define APPLE_ASC_CPU_CONTROL_RUN BIT(4)
+
+/* From the CPU control register. Both bits wait for the firmware's last message to be read. */
+#define APPLE_ASC_CPU_STATUS 4
+#define APPLE_ASC_CPU_STATUS_STOPPED BIT(1)
+#define APPLE_ASC_CPU_STATUS_IDLE BIT(5)
+#define APPLE_ASC_CPU_STATUS_DOWN (APPLE_ASC_CPU_STATUS_STOPPED | APPLE_ASC_CPU_STATUS_IDLE)
+
 #define APPLE_RTKIT_MIN_SUPPORTED_VERSION 11
 #define APPLE_RTKIT_MAX_SUPPORTED_VERSION 12
 
@@ -287,7 +301,7 @@ static void apple_rtkit_management_rx_epmap(struct apple_rtkit *rtk, u64 msg)
 	 * For co-processors that place their own buffers, the crashlog
 	 * endpoint is started before the map is acknowledged.
 	 */
-	if (apple_rtkit_coproc_places_buffers(rtk) && base == 0 &&
+	if (!rtk->warm_restart && apple_rtkit_coproc_places_buffers(rtk) && base == 0 &&
 	    test_bit(APPLE_RTKIT_EP_CRASHLOG, &bitmap)) {
 		ret = apple_rtkit_start_ep(rtk, APPLE_RTKIT_EP_CRASHLOG);
 		if (ret) {
@@ -302,17 +316,46 @@ static void apple_rtkit_management_rx_epmap(struct apple_rtkit *rtk, u64 msg)
 		return;
 	}
 
+	/* macOS's secure side sends each part back unchanged. */
+	if (rtk->port_only) {
+		apple_rtkit_management_send(rtk, APPLE_RTKIT_MGMT_EPMAP_REPLY, msg);
+		if (msg & APPLE_RTKIT_MGMT_EPMAP_LAST) {
+			rtk->boot_result = 0;
+			complete_all(&rtk->epmap_completion);
+		}
+		return;
+	}
+
 	reply = FIELD_PREP(APPLE_RTKIT_MGMT_EPMAP_BASE, base);
 	if (msg & APPLE_RTKIT_MGMT_EPMAP_LAST)
 		reply |= APPLE_RTKIT_MGMT_EPMAP_LAST;
 	else
 		reply |= APPLE_RTKIT_MGMT_EPMAP_REPLY_MORE;
 
+	/*
+	 * macOS's reply to a restarted DCP echoes the map, and adds bit 1 to the
+	 * first part: 0x51d becomes 0x51f. It starts no endpoints. Measured under
+	 * aura, 3 of 3. Why bit 1 isn't known, but ep 1 is the crash log's.
+	 */
+	if (rtk->warm_restart) {
+		if (msg & APPLE_RTKIT_MGMT_EPMAP_LAST)
+			reply |= bitmap & rtk->warm_last_mask;
+		else
+			reply |= bitmap | BIT_ULL(1);
+	}
+
 	apple_rtkit_management_send(rtk, APPLE_RTKIT_MGMT_EPMAP_REPLY, reply);
 
 	if (!(msg & APPLE_RTKIT_MGMT_EPMAP_LAST))
 		return;
 
+	if (rtk->warm_restart) {
+		rtk->warm_restart = false;
+		rtk->boot_result = 0;
+		complete_all(&rtk->epmap_completion);
+		return;
+	}
+
 	for_each_set_bit(ep, rtk->endpoints, APPLE_RTKIT_APP_ENDPOINT_START) {
 		switch (ep) {
 		/* the management endpoint is started by default */
@@ -540,10 +583,14 @@ static void apple_rtkit_free_buffer(struct apple_rtkit *rtk,
 	if (bfr->size == 0)
 		return;
 
-	if (rtk->ops->shmem_destroy)
+	if (bfr == &rtk->crashlog_buffer && rtk->crashlog_placed) {
+		memunmap(bfr->buffer);
+		rtk->crashlog_placed = false;
+	} else if (rtk->ops->shmem_destroy) {
 		rtk->ops->shmem_destroy(rtk->cookie, bfr);
-	else if (bfr->buffer)
+	} else if (bfr->buffer) {
 		dma_free_coherent(rtk->dev, bfr->size, bfr->buffer, bfr->iova);
+	}
 
 	bfr->buffer = NULL;
 	bfr->iomem = NULL;
@@ -775,6 +822,46 @@ static void apple_rtkit_memcpy(struct apple_rtkit *rtk, void *dst,
 						 len, DMA_FROM_DEVICE);
 }
 
+/*
+ * The buffer a crashing firmware placed at its own address, mapped if its IOMMU
+ * translates every page of it to one contiguous range, and acknowledged as a placed
+ * buffer is. The firmware doesn't run again until a reboot.
+ */
+static int apple_rtkit_crashlog_map(struct apple_rtkit *rtk, u64 msg)
+{
+	struct apple_rtkit_shmem *bfr = &rtk->crashlog_buffer;
+	struct iommu_domain *domain = iommu_get_domain_for_dev(rtk->dev);
+	dma_addr_t iova = FIELD_GET(APPLE_RTKIT_BUFFER_REQUEST_IOVA, msg);
+	size_t size = FIELD_GET(APPLE_RTKIT_BUFFER_REQUEST_SIZE, msg) << 12;
+	phys_addr_t phys, page;
+	size_t off;
+	void *va;
+
+	if (!domain || !iova || !size)
+		return -ENODEV;
+	phys = iommu_iova_to_phys(domain, iova);
+	for (off = 0; phys && off < size; off += PAGE_SIZE) {
+		page = iommu_iova_to_phys(domain, iova + off);
+		if (page != phys + off) {
+			dev_err(rtk->dev, "RTKit: crash log at %pad isn't contiguous at +%#zx\n",
+				&iova, off);
+			return -EINVAL;
+		}
+	}
+	if (!phys)
+		return -ENOENT;
+	va = memremap(phys, size, MEMREMAP_WB);
+	if (!va)
+		return -ENOMEM;
+	bfr->buffer = va;
+	bfr->size = size;
+	bfr->iova = iova;
+	bfr->is_mapped = true;
+	rtk->crashlog_placed = true;
+
+	return apple_rtkit_send_message(rtk, APPLE_RTKIT_EP_CRASHLOG, msg, NULL, false);
+}
+
 static void apple_rtkit_crashlog_rx(struct apple_rtkit *rtk, u64 msg)
 {
 	u8 type = FIELD_GET(APPLE_RTKIT_SYSLOG_TYPE, msg);
@@ -787,6 +874,25 @@ static void apple_rtkit_crashlog_rx(struct apple_rtkit *rtk, u64 msg)
 		return;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * A firmware that never announced this endpoint asks for its buffer only when
+	 * it crashes, so there's no handshake to take the request for. Report the
+	 * crash now, as the log may never come.
+	 */
+	if (!rtk->crashlog_buffer.size && !test_bit(APPLE_RTKIT_EP_CRASHLOG, rtk->endpoints)) {
+		int ret = apple_rtkit_crashlog_map(rtk, msg);
+
+		dev_err(rtk->dev, "RTKit: co-processor has crashed: crash log buffer at %#llx %s (%d)\n",
+			FIELD_GET(APPLE_RTKIT_BUFFER_REQUEST_IOVA, msg),
+			ret ? "not mapped" : "mapped, waiting for its log", ret);
+		if (!rtk->crashed) {
+			rtk->crashed = true;
+			if (rtk->ops->crashed)
+				rtk->ops->crashed(rtk->cookie, NULL, 0);
+		}
+		return;
+	}
+
 	if (!rtk->crashlog_buffer.size) {
 		if (apple_rtkit_common_rx_get_buffer(rtk, &rtk->crashlog_buffer,
 						     APPLE_RTKIT_EP_CRASHLOG, msg))
@@ -829,6 +935,9 @@ static void apple_rtkit_crashlog_rx(struct apple_rtkit *rtk, u64 msg)
 			"RTKit: Couldn't allocate crashlog shadow buffer\n");
 	}
 
+	/* Its driver heard of the crash when the buffer was asked for. */
+	if (rtk->crashed)
+		return;
 	rtk->crashed = true;
 	if (rtk->ops->crashed)
 		rtk->ops->crashed(rtk->cookie, bfr, bfr ? rtk->crashlog_buffer.size : 0);
@@ -987,6 +1096,22 @@ static void apple_rtkit_oslog_rx(struct apple_rtkit *rtk, u64 msg)
 	u8 type = FIELD_GET(APPLE_RTKIT_OSLOG_TYPE, msg);
 	int err;
 
+	/*
+	 * A firmware counts its log writes and holds the next counter until the last is
+	 * echoed, and with one outstanding takes no AP power notice: a DCP after a warm
+	 * restart, the MTP and the SMC after about eight sleeps. macOS echoes every one.
+	 */
+	if (type == APPLE_RTKIT_OSLOG_LOG && rtk->oslog_echo) {
+		/* A polled idle that came first echoed it already. */
+		if (cmpxchg(&rtk->oslog_owed, msg, 0) != msg)
+			return;
+		dev_dbg(rtk->dev, "RTKit: oslog counter %016llx, echoed\n", msg);
+		err = apple_rtkit_send_message(rtk, APPLE_RTKIT_EP_OSLOG, msg, NULL, false);
+		if (err)
+			dev_err(rtk->dev, "RTKit: oslog echo failed: %d\n", err);
+		return;
+	}
+
 	/* Preserve the tested Neo/M5 and synchronous-log no-ACK policy. */
 	if (type == APPLE_RTKIT_OSLOG_LOG &&
 	    (rtk->sync_syslog_ack || of_machine_is_compatible("apple,t8140") ||
@@ -1088,12 +1213,17 @@ out:
 	kfree(rtk_work);
 }
 
-static bool apple_rtkit_sync_ack_msg(struct apple_rtkit *rtk, u8 ep, u64 msg)
+/* An OSLOG counter this firmware waits to see echoed. */
+static bool apple_rtkit_oslog_owed_msg(struct apple_rtkit *rtk, u8 ep, u64 msg)
+{
+	return ep == APPLE_RTKIT_EP_OSLOG &&
+	       FIELD_GET(APPLE_RTKIT_OSLOG_TYPE, msg) == APPLE_RTKIT_OSLOG_LOG && rtk->oslog_echo;
+}
+
+static bool apple_rtkit_notification_msg(u8 ep, u64 msg)
 {
 	u8 type = FIELD_GET(APPLE_RTKIT_SYSLOG_TYPE, msg);
 
-	if (!rtk->sync_syslog_ack)
-		return false;
 	if (ep == APPLE_RTKIT_EP_SYSLOG)
 		return type == APPLE_RTKIT_SYSLOG_LOG;
 	if (ep == APPLE_RTKIT_EP_IOREPORT)
@@ -1102,6 +1232,11 @@ static bool apple_rtkit_sync_ack_msg(struct apple_rtkit *rtk, u8 ep, u64 msg)
 	return false;
 }
 
+static bool apple_rtkit_sync_ack_msg(struct apple_rtkit *rtk, u8 ep, u64 msg)
+{
+	return rtk->sync_syslog_ack && apple_rtkit_notification_msg(ep, msg);
+}
+
 static void apple_rtkit_sync_ack_work(struct work_struct *work)
 {
 	struct apple_rtkit *rtk =
@@ -1172,6 +1307,8 @@ static void apple_rtkit_rx(struct apple_mbox *mbox, struct apple_mbox_msg msg,
 	if (!work)
 		return;
 
+	if (apple_rtkit_oslog_owed_msg(rtk, ep, msg.msg0))
+		WRITE_ONCE(rtk->oslog_owed, msg.msg0);
 	work->rtk = rtk;
 	work->ep = ep;
 	work->msg = msg.msg0;
@@ -1217,6 +1354,82 @@ int apple_rtkit_poll(struct apple_rtkit *rtk)
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(apple_rtkit_poll);
 
+static int apple_rtkit_poll_handshake(struct apple_rtkit *rtk, unsigned int timeout_ms)
+{
+	unsigned long timeout = jiffies + msecs_to_jiffies(timeout_ms);
+	int ret;
+
+	do {
+		ret = apple_rtkit_poll(rtk);
+		if (ret < 0)
+			return ret;
+		if (try_wait_for_completion(&rtk->epmap_completion))
+			return rtk->boot_result;
+		usleep_range(50, 100);
+	} while (time_before(jiffies, timeout));
+
+	return try_wait_for_completion(&rtk->epmap_completion) ? rtk->boot_result : -ETIME;
+}
+
+static int apple_rtkit_port_power(struct apple_rtkit *rtk, unsigned int state,
+				  unsigned int timeout_ms)
+{
+	unsigned long timeout = jiffies + msecs_to_jiffies(timeout_ms);
+	int ret;
+
+	reinit_completion(&rtk->ap_pwr_ack_completion);
+	ret = apple_rtkit_management_send(rtk, APPLE_RTKIT_MGMT_SET_AP_PWR_STATE,
+					  FIELD_PREP(APPLE_RTKIT_MGMT_PWR_STATE, state));
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	do {
+		ret = apple_rtkit_poll(rtk);
+		if (ret < 0)
+			return ret;
+		if (try_wait_for_completion(&rtk->ap_pwr_ack_completion))
+			return 0;
+		usleep_range(50, 100);
+	} while (time_before(jiffies, timeout));
+
+	return -ETIME;
+}
+
+int apple_rtkit_port_map(struct apple_rtkit *rtk, unsigned int timeout_ms)
+{
+	rtk->port_only = true;
+
+	return apple_rtkit_poll_handshake(rtk, timeout_ms);
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(apple_rtkit_port_map);
+
+int apple_rtkit_port_on(struct apple_rtkit *rtk, unsigned int timeout_ms)
+{
+	/* The co-processor starts its side of this port itself. */
+	rtk->iop_power_state = APPLE_RTKIT_PWR_STATE_ON;
+
+	return apple_rtkit_port_power(rtk, APPLE_RTKIT_PWR_STATE_INIT, timeout_ms);
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(apple_rtkit_port_on);
+
+int apple_rtkit_port_handshake(struct apple_rtkit *rtk, unsigned int timeout_ms)
+{
+	int ret;
+
+	ret = apple_rtkit_port_map(rtk, timeout_ms);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	return apple_rtkit_port_on(rtk, timeout_ms);
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(apple_rtkit_port_handshake);
+
+int apple_rtkit_port_idle(struct apple_rtkit *rtk, unsigned int timeout_ms)
+{
+	return apple_rtkit_port_power(rtk, APPLE_RTKIT_PWR_STATE_IDLE, timeout_ms);
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(apple_rtkit_port_idle);
+
 void apple_rtkit_hold_rx(struct apple_rtkit *rtk)
 {
 	apple_mbox_hold_rx(rtk->mbox);
@@ -1288,6 +1501,10 @@ static void apple_rtkit_mark_running(struct apple_rtkit *rtk)
 	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(system_endpoints); i++)
 		set_bit(system_endpoints[i], rtk->endpoints);
 
+	/* apple_rtkit_wake() must not wait for an EPMAP that already happened. */
+	rtk->boot_result = 0;
+	complete_all(&rtk->epmap_completion);
+
 	rtk->iop_power_state = APPLE_RTKIT_PWR_STATE_ON;
 	rtk->ap_power_state = APPLE_RTKIT_PWR_STATE_ON;
 	rtk->syslog_inherited = true;
@@ -1427,6 +1644,8 @@ static struct apple_rtkit *__apple_rtkit_init(struct device *dev, void *cookie,
 	rtk->handshake_timeout_ms = APPLE_RTKIT_HANDSHAKE_TIMEOUT_MS;
 	rtk->sync_syslog_ack =
 		of_property_read_bool(dev->of_node, "apple,sync-syslog-ack");
+	/* As macOS does; a driver whose firmware shouldn't get them turns it off. */
+	rtk->oslog_echo = of_machine_is_compatible("apple,t8140");
 	INIT_KFIFO(rtk->sync_ack_fifo);
 	INIT_WORK(&rtk->sync_ack_work, apple_rtkit_sync_ack_work);
 
@@ -1453,6 +1672,8 @@ static struct apple_rtkit *__apple_rtkit_init(struct device *dev, void *cookie,
 		goto free_rtk;
 	}
 
+	rtk->warm_last_mask = U32_MAX;
+
 	rtk->wq = alloc_ordered_workqueue("rtkit-%s", WQ_HIGHPRI | WQ_MEM_RECLAIM,
 					  dev_name(rtk->dev));
 	if (!rtk->wq) {
@@ -1509,13 +1730,16 @@ static int apple_rtkit_wait_for_completion(struct apple_rtkit *rtk,
 /*
  * A retained wake is not interrupted by a signal to its caller: it has
  * already started the co-processor's CPU, and nothing would undo that.
+ * Otherwise a handshake given up halfway can leave a firmware started that
+ * nothing stops. The freezer signals every user task as a sleep begins, so
+ * only a fatal signal ends the wait, and during a system sleep not even that.
  */
 static long apple_rtkit_wait_timeout(struct apple_rtkit *rtk, struct completion *c,
 				     unsigned long timeout)
 {
-	if (READ_ONCE(rtk->retained_wake))
+	if (READ_ONCE(rtk->retained_wake) || pm_suspend_target_state == PM_SUSPEND_MEM)
 		return wait_for_completion_timeout(c, timeout);
-	return wait_for_completion_interruptible_timeout(c, timeout);
+	return wait_for_completion_killable_timeout(c, timeout);
 }
 
 /*
@@ -1529,13 +1753,8 @@ static int apple_rtkit_wait(struct apple_rtkit *rtk, struct completion *c)
 	long t;
 	int ret;
 
-	if (!rtk->sync_syslog_ack) {
-		t = apple_rtkit_wait_timeout(rtk, c,
-					     msecs_to_jiffies(rtk->handshake_timeout_ms));
-		if (t < 0)
-			return t;
-		return t ? 0 : -ETIME;
-	}
